\end{align*}
For $b_n$, we have:
\begin{align*}
b_n &= b_{n - 1} + \frac {b_{n - 1}^2}{b_{n - 2}} \\
\end{align*}
Let's look at the first few terms to identify patterns. For $a_n$, we have:
\begin{align*}
a_2 &= a_1 + \frac {a_1^2}{a_0} = 1 + \frac{1^2}{1} = 2 \\
a_3 &= a_2 + \frac {a_2^2}{a_1} = 2 + \frac{2^2}{1} = 6 \\
a_4 &= a_3 + \frac {a_3^2}{a_2} = 6 + \frac{6^2}{2} = 24 \\
\end{align*}
We can see that $a_n$ seems to be related to factorials. If we look closely, we see that $a_n = (n - 1)!$. This can be proven by induction. Assume that $a_k = (k - 1)!$ for all $k < n$. Then:
\begin{align*}
a_n &= a_{n - 1} + \frac {a_{n - 1}^2}{a_{n - 2}} \\
&= (n - 2)! + \frac{((n - 2)!)^2}{((n - 3)!)} \\
&= (n - 2)! + \frac{(n - 2)!}{(n - 3)!} \\
&= (n - 2)! + (n - 2) \\
